 "conclusion": "fbt cache saturation is real on the heaviest d10 sweep candidates (up to 834,047 dropped queries on cost-73c bit32 at ~89% occupancy) and silently skips blocked-mask OR contributions in the canonical engine. Every saturated row's published REFUTED-D10 verdict was re-solved with FBS=1<<20 (fbtDrops=0 required) and survived unchanged (loss=false, no verdict flips, no WIN7 appearing or disappearing). All eight audited WIN7 rows show zero drops, so no published certificate is affected. The certificate layer is independently drop-guarded (the extractor already requires FBS=1<<20 with fbt_drops=0 on every published certificate). No published record, frontier number, or verdict changes as a result of this audit.",
 "saturation_threshold_observation": "Observed saturation onset: the least-inserted saturated row is cost-74 bit70 (90,581 search-time inserts, i.e. 90,616 total occupancy = 69.1% of 131,072 slots); the most-inserted clean row is cost-74 bit20 (87,124 inserts = 66.5% occupancy). All rows below ~87k inserts were clean; all rows above ~90.6k saturated. fbtMaxProbe reached the 47/48 probe ceiling exactly on the saturated rows.",
 "recommendation": "Sweep candidates expected above ~19M nodes (roughly >=90k fbt inserts) should be run with the drop counter; any saturated verdict must be confirmed with the FBS=1<<20 re-solve before being reused as evidence. The canonical runner stays byte-identical for raw comparability. Remaining unaudited >=19M-node rows from earlier sweeps (e.g. cost-65b/66a/66b/68/68b/70b heaviest rows) are cheap follow-ups.",
